In a **dot plot**, read the values left to right, counting each dot as one value, so a stack of $2$ dots counts twice.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{arrows.meta}
\draw[-{Stealth}] (-0.4,0) -- (8.6,0);
\foreach \x in {0,...,8} {\draw (\x,-0.12) -- (\x,0.12);}
\foreach \x/\c in {0/1,1/2,3/1,4/2,6/1,7/1} {\foreach \k in {1,...,\c} {\fill (\x,{0.45*\k}) circle (3pt);}}
\node[above] at (4.0,1.5) {one dot $=$ one data value};
\draw[-{Stealth}] (0.3,-0.8) -- (7.7,-0.8);
\node[below] at (4.0,-0.9) {values increase in this direction};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

In an **ordered stem-and-leaf plot**, each row's leaves are in ascending order and the stems increase down the page. Check this before finding the summary, and order any unordered leaves first.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\node at (0.7,0.7) {Stem};
\node at (2.9,0.7) {Leaf};
\draw (0,0.35) -- (4.8,0.35);
\draw (1.4,0.5) -- (1.4,-2.1);
\node at (0.7,-0.05) {$1$};
\node at (0.7,-0.75) {$2$};
\node at (0.7,-1.45) {$3$};
\foreach \x/\v in {1.9/2, 2.5/5, 3.1/8} {\node at (\x,-0.05) {$\v$};}
\foreach \x/\v in {1.9/0, 2.5/3, 3.1/3, 3.7/7, 4.3/9} {\node at (\x,-0.75) {$\v$};}
\foreach \x/\v in {1.9/1, 2.5/4} {\node at (\x,-1.45) {$\v$};}
\node at (2.4,-2.5) {every row is already in order};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

